From what I've been tracking on HEX, there are three areas that would give us the biggest payoff in CFD turnaround, reruns, and HPC time. I've got a fuller write-up I can send over; here's the quick version.

The overall gain is getting CFD out of work that isn't ours and out of complexity we don't need. That means weeks back on geometry, fewer reruns from mismatched or outdated models, and fewer full CFD cases per delivery, with savings that grow over time. It also lets other teams bring us in earlier instead of working around us as the bottleneck.

1. CAD-owned CFD geometry. CAD engineers maintain a clean, import-ready CFD model in a central repo, and we review and approve. Cleanup alone took one to two months on HEX, and this keeps everyone on the same geometry.

2. Histograms for GenHealth. Instead of cutting geometry into panels, we export histogram reports (averaging preserved) and GenHealth post-processes them their way. It already works today; we just need to adopt it.

3. Surrogate modeling for square-matrix requests. Run a targeted subset and fill the rest with GPR. This cut my case count by a third on HEX. I'm putting together a short course for the team.

